    <node id="Regulation">
      <data key="d0">Regulation</data>
      <data key="d1">Regulation</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation refers to the legal framework that governs the classification of AI systems based on their risk level and conformity assessment procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulation outlines obligations for AI models and systems, especially those with systemic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulation provides specific rules governing the use and market placement of General-Purpose AI Models, especially those posing systemic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulation refers to a comprehensive legal framework established by the European Union (EU) aimed at governing the development, deployment, and utilization of artificial intelligence (AI) systems within the EU market. It serves multiple critical objectives, including enhancing the functioning of the internal market, promoting the adoption of trustworthy AI, and safeguarding health and fundamental rights.

Central to this Regulation is its focus on high-risk categories of AI systems, particularly those used in medical devices and biometric identification within law enforcement contexts. The Regulation imposes stringent obligations on the deployers of high-risk AI systems to ensure transparency, proper functioning, and compliance with safety standards. It addresses various systemic risks associated with these technologies, requiring AI providers to adhere to intellectual property rights, consult with workers regarding AI deployments, and adhere to ethical standards.

The Regulation also mandates that EU Member States designate supervisory authorities responsible for overseeing AI activities, which includes establishing guidelines for incident reporting and fostering collaboration with experts to enhance oversight. A key aspect of this Regulation is its commitment to public safety, as it specifies conditions for the use of biometric identification systems and aims to improve working conditions in platform work environments.

Furthermore, the Regulation outlines a phased compliance approach, allowing for voluntary adherence until August 2, 2025, after which enforceable requirements will be implemented. This approach is designed to align with existing data protection legislation and solidify AI governance within the EU.

Moreover, the Regulation specifies requirements for registering high-risk AI systems and managing the EU database that tracks these systems. It promotes the harmonization of AI practices across EU Member States and details frameworks for evaluations and reporting mechanisms.

Upon its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union, the Regulation will establish effective dates for its provisions and a funding framework aimed at fostering innovation in AI while prioritizing ethical standards and safety protocols. The framework will also include enforcement measures, such as penalties for non-compliance, and protective provisions for whistleblowers to ensure accountability and transparency within the industry. Overall, the Regulation aims to create a uniform legal framework for the development and use of AI systems throughout the Union, while ensuring health, safety, and the protection of fundamental rights.</data>
