      <data key="d0">high-risk</data>
      <data key="d1">Risk</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk indicates the potential danger associated with the decisions made by emergency healthcare patient triage systems, particularly in life-critical scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8c0eb33a64c460fe6d54eafa4e0eb01</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_58.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857235</data>
    </node>
    <node id="decisions">
      <data key="d0">decisions</data>
      <data key="d1">QualityMeasurement</data>
      <data key="d2">Decisions made in emergency healthcare patient triage systems involve assessing patient conditions and prioritizing care based on severity and urgency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8c0eb33a64c460fe6d54eafa4e0eb01</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_58.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857235</data>
    </node>
    <node id="life and health of persons">
      <data key="d0">life and health of persons</data>
      <data key="d1">Impact</data>
      <data key="d2">The life and health of persons are significantly impacted by the effectiveness and accuracy of emergency healthcare patient triage systems' decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8c0eb33a64c460fe6d54eafa4e0eb01</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_58.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857235</data>
    </node>
    <node id="property">
      <data key="d0">property</data>
      <data key="d1">Impact</data>
      <data key="d2">Property refers to possessions or assets that can be impacted by the outcomes of decisions made by triage systems, especially in emergencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8c0eb33a64c460fe6d54eafa4e0eb01</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_58.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857235</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI systems">
      <data key="d0">AI systems</data>
      <data key="d1">AISystem</data>
      <data key="d2">AI systems are advanced computational frameworks that integrate both software and hardware components to perform a variety of tasks typically requiring human intelligence, such as learning and decision-making. These systems can operate under regulatory oversight and are utilized in diverse applications, including biometric verification, which involves authenticating individuals by matching their identities against claimed identities. 

While AI systems represent significant advancements in computational capabilities, they also pose potential risks to health and safety, particularly when used as safety components within products. Moreover, they may exhibit biases that could adversely affect individuals' rights, leading to discriminatory outcomes such as social scoring of natural persons. 

As such, there is a pressing need for safeguards and ethical guidelines to manage AI technologies effectively and prevent discrimination. Overall, while AI systems offer immense potential for innovation and efficiency, careful management is essential to mitigate the associated risks and ensure their responsible use.&lt;SEP&gt;AI systems are computational systems designed to perform tasks that typically require human intelligence, potentially including data processing.&lt;SEP&gt;AI systems are automated processes designed to perform tasks typically requiring human intelligence, subjected to regulations in the Union.&lt;SEP&gt;AI systems are technologies that can be deployed across various sectors of the economy and society, enabling tasks that range from processing data to making decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;AI systems refer to artificial intelligence technology that must adhere to specific rules and regulations for market deployment and usage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-63ef16386f45c7bc364061cc094254fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae8bdbb57af24d78fe2313201571bea7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a2f07f347fedfe788b6636feb212d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5ee58307a02a795e806984f0ff4abe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d7d6f2dd7e28584da10fdb340b37fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28efea7f8fe5945cce5b7be57e88efe7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfc036b3a79fdcd1eb36c0af6aafbb4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cac929d0048fe34377179e8ecde4a5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602b68fe088482c0037845fc0b520c30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18cf1aa30864348514b20fe64038fba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-649ac2acd7b1a3b0b6a81ce20aa799f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62c8c9618d935cf8910927a7d518c1f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13991bdab077b3114e8913f89a1371e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0bd7056742f766183c11176d91fb1e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5928be36056ac91865bd3e4d596159e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_47.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_70.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_10.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_54.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_15.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_31.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_1.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_118.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_3.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="general-purpose AI models">
      <data key="d0">general-purpose AI models</data>
      <data key="d1">GPAIModel</data>
      <data key="d2">General-purpose AI models are AI systems that are designed to perform a wide range of tasks across various domains and could present systemic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;General-purpose AI models are versatile AI systems designed to be adaptable for a wide range of applications and domains. These models are intended for various uses while complying with market regulations and safety standards. Evaluation of their capabilities and associated risks is essential, and such models require ongoing monitoring and governance to ensure compliance. Overall, general-purpose AI models demonstrate significant versatility in their applications across different fields while adhering to the regulatory frameworks established by governing bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;General-purpose AI models are those that can be used for a wide array of applications, subject to regulation for safe market entry.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfc036b3a79fdcd1eb36c0af6aafbb4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ce3f90343791af10ffae2a7848f26e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-649ac2acd7b1a3b0b6a81ce20aa799f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13991bdab077b3114e8913f89a1371e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b16c22014fb75795531a9a187b8fc81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5928be36056ac91865bd3e4d596159e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_101.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_1.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union harmonisation legislation">
      <data key="d0">Union harmonisation legislation</data>
      <data key="d1">Regulation</data>
      <data key="d2">Union harmonisation legislation aims to facilitate the free movement of compliant products within the internal market while ensuring safety standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Union harmonisation legislation outlines the legal framework that AI systems must adhere to within the Union, particularly regarding high-risk classifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Union harmonisation legislation refers to laws that regulate trade and product safety across Union member states and influence high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Union harmonisation legislation governs the placing of high-risk AI systems on the market, mandating compliance with technical documentation standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c9021d6cb53dceeddd1e648d3e37fbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18cf1aa30864348514b20fe64038fba8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13991bdab077b3114e8913f89a1371e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36cb0fd5abf1b169a8aa13021f58c7b2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_11.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_47.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_25.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863776</data>
    </node>
    <node id="high-risk AI systems">
      <data key="d0">high-risk AI systems</data>
      <data key="d1">AIModel</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk AI systems are AI applications identified as potentially having significant impact on health, safety, or rights.&lt;SEP&gt;High-risk AI systems are advanced technologies identified as posing significant risks that necessitate additional oversight and compliance measures. These systems are carefully regulated due to their potential implications on fundamental rights, safety, and privacy, especially in sensitive sectors such as law enforcement, migration, and financial services. Recognized under specific regulatory frameworks, high-risk AI systems require stringent management and scrutiny, similar to those applied to medical devices, ensuring they meet particular compliance requirements.

These systems are characterized by their complexity and the necessity of adherence to conformity assessment procedures. Providers of high-risk AI systems are obligated to follow stricter protocols, particularly during transitional periods, to mitigate any potential threats posed by these technologies. Moreover, these AI systems must perform consistently for their intended purposes and include specific risk management measures to protect health, safety, and fundamental rights.

In summary, the classification of high-risk AI systems reflects a comprehensive approach aimed at ensuring their safe deployment and regulation within critical sectors of society, thereby managing the significant potential harms and legal concerns associated with their use.&lt;SEP&gt;High-risk AI systems are those that pose substantial risks to health, safety, and fundamental rights as defined in regulatory context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c9021d6cb53dceeddd1e648d3e37fbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5ee58307a02a795e806984f0ff4abe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f12c01099dadb1fc1008ce18c7d800e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69838403e8b59bb122436a0d25a9fc1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5644d48de211c02e29152f909c69640b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ef16386f45c7bc364061cc094254fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70337c5367702b9a8ef4dd8a241aa46d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a68b9cb2eff595118d2af9313b6c89f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602b68fe088482c0037845fc0b520c30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13991bdab077b3114e8913f89a1371e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe49d95d50900e82a706b2766339562f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d7d6f2dd7e28584da10fdb340b37fd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54948128ea5a4b84a219d7dbaa346a50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc1203d6797827fbb6dbb7dc7c8d7d26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ca7c773df7242c37f506b8ad3db475b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a57bbcc3ccf00f26352f12c36036aedf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-595952fa3e7e47cb312adaaad303b73c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8c5a915dca180e1c423cefa27ea6cd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5928be36056ac91865bd3e4d596159e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_43.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_70.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_14.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_10.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_36.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_125.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_178.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_54.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_84.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_25.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_155.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_1.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_9.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_74.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_7.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863895</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Member States">
      <data key="d0">Member States</data>
      <data key="d1">Stakeholder</data>
      <data key="d2">Member States are countries that are part of the European Union and are involved in the regulatory processes of EU law.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States are involved in the regulatory framework, receiving notifications and reports about notified bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States are national bodies that cooperate with the Commission on AI regulation and conformity measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States refer to the individual countries that comprise the European Union (EU), and they play a crucial role in the development, implementation, and enforcement of regulations concerning artificial intelligence (AI) systems. These entities possess defined powers and competencies within the framework of EU regulations, particularly with respect to AI policy implementation. 

Each Member State is responsible for applying EU regulations related to AI and reporting on the performance of AI systems within its jurisdiction. They are encouraged to support and promote the development of AI solutions that yield socially and environmentally beneficial outcomes. In this capacity, Member States recognize fundamental rights, including essential labor rights such as the right to strike and negotiate collective agreements, thereby ensuring that their regulatory framework considers the implications for the workforce.

Member States actively participate in shaping the regulatory environment for AI by engaging in consultations and evaluations concerning national compliance measures with EU directives. Their responsibilities also extend to promoting AI literacy, crafting initiatives, and establishing codes of conduct for AI usage. They hold the authority to manage the authorization process for high-risk AI systems, including the right to raise objections that protect their jurisdictional interests. To enforce AI regulations effectively, Member States are required to establish national competent authorities that oversee compliance and manage AI regulatory sandboxes aimed at fostering innovation while adhering to regulations.

In the case of non-compliance with regulatory standards, Member States define appropriate penalties and are tasked with ensuring their enforcement within specified timelines. They provide expertise to the EU Council and Commission to influence legislative decisions and represent their interests in AI regulatory discussions. This involves selecting chairpersons and balancing the need for compliance with the necessity to promote technological advancement.

Collaboration among Member States is fundamental for achieving a unified approach to AI regulation across Europe. They engage in agreements that facilitate information exchange while maintaining confidentiality, especially concerning national security and defense activities linked to compliance. Additionally, Member States implement national funding programs and projects aligned with the principles established by the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities.

Ultimately, Member States play an essential role in shaping the EU's regulatory landscape for AI. They enhance collective expertise and capabilities, ensuring compliance and reporting of incidents involving high-risk AI systems within their jurisdictions. They may also institute more stringent laws regarding the use of remote biometric identification systems and high-risk AI systems as permitted by Union law while promoting innovation and safeguarding public interests. Furthermore, they are responsible for identifying and maintaining a list of public authorities for compliance and are evaluated as entities that can designate relevant authorities for market surveillance in accordance with EU regulations, thus ensuring transparency and&lt;SEP&gt;Member States are national representatives that must be informed about evaluations and actions concerning AI systems that cross borders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8caee37338a6383c30fe06ea2b3e4761&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b43f399350f0fc36795c0ce358459d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba662546e18e6e5f82c3cd1c5b4c3b75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b235644744fa597c94328afbeaa7e56b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f12c01099dadb1fc1008ce18c7d800e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e78bce1eaa5df9c186492b5e1482218&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a90a47461f525bba18d74c42ea04f88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad9c740a10a5c1577e578a593bd61fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7912c5a4fb8a11a998bec401999088b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7365f713d09e74d49090cb78cb3e19b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0711090702fd9b1787822998d5deac9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f892644f7a3eab6b024d8a386777f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3105f8ed20ed5608a4571fe7b1d8f942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ef16386f45c7bc364061cc094254fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22275ba6343521309923fef233affa2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ca16d466deaa751e3b9dc390e91e2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fa5fe9ac042448be58de6c82bb33ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577494efd5ce842563e6974b0da9707b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40f3761e589fb7702938fd4fbbecfdcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0789803641ff590acdf82b97e7d4afbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86aff1d72a3f6226d0b5db29d9910a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5981afe4b9fb547c64f49e76b1d9c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-244204be180c4ed9731f434ed67ee99b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43942ba7283893ebf128acb858a20c58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-064e3e5273a81cd8d9a47da9a3f51240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b837dd750c95d041180fa3d735328672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72498ec90c19419f10373dde7e7b51b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82b1b08be1175d2e6d49ab5a294ef86a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c76dd7edbc6dcf218d6558d18116d52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f94df6da4de89cee24b1bd8f504c377&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4988a00689afb1245e0f0ce4ceef5921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9317b056c2a260423c79e368903cde7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c742eff7a2b610bb01c8c0ec233b62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a2f07f347fedfe788b6636feb212d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdf1c7e7ad7fe2c6b1b8f1b0b12db35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475967c83e624bc711a9ff13e8150163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40a9e2a0fc4601f4b3d4f583384b6a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0dc307b8b7cb1f0e39aa9b4b1839c408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2806e288e0b71