    <node id="Articles 53 and 55">
      <data key="d0">Articles 53 and 55</data>
      <data key="d1">Documentation</data>
      <data key="d2">Articles 53 and 55 lay down obligations for participants under the regulation, impacting their adherence to codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Articles 53 and 55 outline specific obligations and requirements that must be fulfilled under the Regulation regarding AI operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Codes of Practice">
      <data key="d0">Codes of Practice</data>
      <data key="d1">Documentation</data>
      <data key="d2">Codes of Practice are comprehensive guidelines designed to help entities, particularly AI model providers, fulfill their obligations related to the detection and labeling of manipulated content, subject to approval by the Commission. These codes aim to ensure compliance with various regulations specifically surrounding AI technologies, effectively addressing numerous AI-related obligations. 

They outline specific objectives, commitments, and key performance indicators that facilitate the implementation of AI technologies and are directed towards a broad range of stakeholders, including civil society and industry. The Codes of Practice establish standards and obligations that AI model providers must adhere to, ensuring that safety and ethical practices are upheld during the deployment of AI systems.

Encouraged by the AI Office, these codes play a crucial role in promoting effective implementation of AI regulations at the Union level, ultimately supporting compliance with obligations associated with AI content labeling. Additionally, they serve as guidelines for AI providers to demonstrate compliance with obligations related to systemic risks and to meet regulatory requirements by specified deadlines. Overall, the Codes of Practice are essential tools designed to enhance transparency and accountability within the realm of AI deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of practice serve as a tool for compliance with obligations under the Regulation for providers of general-purpose AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c8a6b92fbe8d93eb878957c5ed60aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51cb0d95dff3b7f033b351a9352b26a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50cb89ed7d0d818018d373ebefa65267&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40a9e2a0fc4601f4b3d4f583384b6a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c49e78c25e30ed3a2557915010f14f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18d33fd192f06f727ac6bb21becbaa57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3034aa8ff1b6d41ac6e0b8ff655b76ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0373d38f7600f51def3b5e5cb5061d17</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_135.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_117.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_89.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_179.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_53.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863523</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stakeholders">
      <data key="d0">Stakeholders</data>
      <data key="d1">Stakeholder</data>
      <data key="d2">Stakeholders are defined as individuals or groups who play a significant role in the context of high-risk AI systems and their deployment. They are entities who are directly affected by the impacts of AI technologies, and their insights are sought during impact assessments to ensure a comprehensive understanding of potential outcomes. The stakeholder group encompasses a broad spectrum, including civil society organizations, industry representatives, academia, research organizations, trade unions, small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), standardization organizations, and consumer protection groups, all of whom possess a vested interest in the implementation and regulation of AI.

These stakeholders are not only involved in the development and oversight of AI systems but also represent various actors within the AI value chain. They are encouraged to actively engage in AI literacy initiatives to promote compliance and safety in AI practices. Furthermore, stakeholders play a crucial role in the effective coordination and implementation of AI regulations, ensuring that all relevant parties are involved in the governance and development of AI systems and applications. Their participation is essential for fostering discussions and promoting a shared understanding among different interest groups related to AI.&lt;SEP&gt;Stakeholders refer to individuals or organizations that have an interest or involvement in the measures and standards applied to high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Stakeholders include organizations and individuals who are involved in the development and deployment of AI systems, focusing on risk mitigation and performance communication.&lt;SEP&gt;Stakeholders are individuals or organizations that are directly affected or have an interest in the legal requirements established by Regulation (EU) 2019/1020.&lt;SEP&gt;Stakeholders are individuals or groups that have an interest in the development and implementation of standards, including SMEs, consumer organizations, and environmental groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-849f8f0403e6b12fa8a9c24b84c094c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21acb22d994b8f65ea3709a99eb85032&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c14e1756e1d8d71ac3ba07125f122da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e28e08bfb7b1ae3208e2c91ea6f1e4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad86065dee426d0a1db7444552c4a385&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671e76af4a78aebc96c988f0504bfa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-699c5fa3a4d8d093e6a599637df82443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a811acaf13b6420a717f6aa21a6ed331&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a1d78baf29c07c65edc311f0ca04126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40de45b5b0c24b78b7ef2ca4e835c29c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24261c10195e33e965bd7e966c33337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20ac675fffdeba0fbc242b392d3af8da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-256a6003a5841f2764536c55f7086431&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2739984aae71aa27a0af867bc197b730</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_121.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_15.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_165.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_27.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_67.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_74.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_73.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_96.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_20.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863576</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Key Performance Indicators">
      <data key="d0">Key Performance Indicators</data>
      <data key="d1">Metric</data>
      <data key="d2">Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) are metrics defined to measure the achievement of objectives within the context of codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Key performance indicators are metrics used to measure the achievement and effectiveness of commitments made under the codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Key Performance Indicators are measurable values that assess the success of objectives related to AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Key Performance Indicators are measurable values that demonstrate how effectively objectives are being achieved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-064e3e5273a81cd8d9a47da9a3f51240&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a811acaf13b6420a717f6aa21a6ed331</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_165.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_95.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750858963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assessment and Management Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Assessment and Management Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">AICapability</data>
      <data key="d2">Assessment and management procedures are the processes involved in identifying and mitigating systemic risks associated with AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Documentation Requirements">
      <data key="d0">Documentation Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">Documentation</data>
      <data key="d2">Documentation requirements stipulate the need for comprehensive records outlining how AI systems operate and the management of risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Implementing Acts">
      <data key="d0">Implementing Acts</data>
      <data key="d1">Regulation</data>
      <data key="d2">Implementing Acts are specific legal instruments adopted by the European Commission to give effect to the provisions set out in various regulations. These acts serve multiple functions, including the approval of codes of practice and establishing common rules for the implementation of laws. They contain detailed arrangements and procedural safeguards, particularly concerning the imposition of fines. Additionally, Implementing Acts specify common rules for obligations related to content detection and labeling. Moreover, they play a crucial role in governing the establishment and operation of AI regulatory sandboxes, ensuring that the regulations, such as those in Regulation (EU) 2018/1139, are executed effectively in practice. Overall, Implementing Acts are essential regulatory measures facilitating the practical application of legal frameworks within the European Union.&lt;SEP&gt;Implementing acts are legal texts adopted by the Commission to enforce regulations regarding AI systems and standards compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;Implementing acts are measures adopted by the Commission to ensure the uniform application of EU legislation, particularly in AI regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-010f171cc1a7f217689efe00fd8cb656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de6a0d59bb86cca6093fbb52cf156b4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f41337092de45470ab838aff17309ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9e2499910b26136c66e6e7a013d8f0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7805654ce0fe0d8d9b6ba008562d2aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3034aa8ff1b6d41ac6e0b8ff655b76ec</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_109.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_41.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_101.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_108.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750862836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emerging Standards">
      <data key="d0">Emerging Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">Standard</data>
      <data key="d2">Emerging standards are new benchmarks being developed in response to advancements in technology and AI ethics.&lt;SEP&gt;Emerging standards represent new guidelines or criteria that the AI Office may consider when reviewing the codes of practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Civil Society Organisations">
      <data key="d0">Civil Society Organisations</data>
      <data key="d1">Stakeholder</data>
      <data key="d2">Civil society organisations are relevant stakeholders that may support the implementation of codes of practice in the AI sector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750857367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent Experts">
      <data key="d0">Independent Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">AISubject</data>
      <data key="d2">Independent Experts are individuals or organizations involved by the AI Office to conduct evaluations to ensure compliance with regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are stakeholders who provide unbiased advice and support in the development and evaluation of codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent Experts are involved in the evaluation procedures of AI models as stipulated by the Commission's implementing acts.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts appointed by the Commission conduct evaluations on behalf of the Commission regarding AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fcd3af92cad1d85ebf561f059acb114</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_164.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_92.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1750863853</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industry">
      <data key="d0">Industry</data>
      <data key="d1">Stakeholder</data>
      <data key="d2">Industry refers to organizations and companies involved in the development and deployment of AI models, influencing regulatory practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Industry represents commercial stakeholders who have interests in the regulatory framework and its applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Industry stakeholders include businesses and organizations that engage in AI technologies and are affected by the codes of practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882</data>
