    <edge source="Member States" target="Commission">
      <data key="d6">129.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Commission and Member States engage in a collaborative relationship focused on the regulation and governance of artificial intelligence (AI) systems throughout Europe. This partnership is instrumental in creating a regulatory framework that benefits all stakeholders, particularly small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). Member States play a critical role by submitting feedback and influencing regulatory guidelines initiated by the Commission. Additionally, they are responsible for reporting their use of remote biometric identification systems through annual updates, ensuring compliance and transparency in line with EU laws.

The Commission actively supports Member States and SMEs in understanding and adhering to AI regulations, which includes providing access to expert resources. This effort aims to mitigate compliance costs and enhance the practical application of regulatory measures. The Commission remains flexible, updating regulations as necessary—either in response to requests from Member States or as independent updates—to stay aligned with the rapidly evolving landscape of AI technologies.

To ensure uniformity in the application of regulations across Europe, the Commission coordinates closely with Member States. This includes sharing reports and facilitating communication regarding decisions, especially in cases where objections to authorizations occur or when evaluations of national measures are needed. The overall collaboration between the Commission and Member States is crucial for establishing a well-regulated environment that fosters the development and implementation of AI technologies across the continent.</data>
      <data key="d8">collaboration,collaborative governance,compliance,compliance uniformity,consultation,cooperation,coordination,evaluation,facilitation,governance,guidance,implementation,information sharing,organizational support,oversight,regulation,regulatory collaboration,regulatory compliance,regulatory cooperation,regulatory framework,regulatory process,regulatory updates,stakeholder influence,stakeholder involvement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9317b056c2a260423c79e368903cde7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8caee37338a6383c30fe06ea2b3e4761&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c742eff7a2b610bb01c8c0ec233b62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b235644744fa597c94328afbeaa7e56b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fa5fe9ac042448be58de6c82bb33ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f79a9fca1518856b24fdcb7e59f040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0789803641ff590acdf82b97e7d4afbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2806e288e0b71013209c4ad85ecaa38b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-244204be180c4ed9731f434ed67ee99b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-595952fa3e7e47cb312adaaad303b73c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21acb22d994b8f65ea3709a99eb85032&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea6a588c10ad2fe493ec8cc1913f72d</data>
